msgid "Observability Dev Services with Grafana OTel LGTM"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This Dev Service provides the https://github.com/grafana/docker-otel-"
"lgtm[Grafana OTel-LGTM], an `all-in-one` Docker image containing an https://"
"opentelemetry.io/docs/collector[OpenTelemetry Collector] receiving and then "
"forwarding telemetry data to Prometheus (metrics), Tempo (traces) and Loki "
"(logs).\n"
"This data can then be visualized by https://github.com/grafana/"
"grafana[Grafana]. The LGTM abbreviation stands for:"
msgstr ""

msgid "L -> https://github.com/grafana/loki[Loki] (logs)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"G -> https://github.com/grafana/grafana[Grafana] (metrics visualization)"
msgstr ""

msgid "T -> https://github.com/grafana/tempo[Tempo] (traces)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"M -> https://grafana.com/oss/mimir[Mimir] (long term storage for Prometheus)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Configuring your project"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you are using `quarkus-opentelemetry` or `quarkus-micrometer-"
"opentelemetry` this Observability extension is already bundled and the "
"container will start automatically in dev mode."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If the mentioned extensions are not present, you can manually add the "
"Quarkus Grafana OTel LGTM sink (where data goes)\n"
"extension to your build file:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Micrometer"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The https://quarkus.io/guides/telemetry-micrometer[Micrometer Quarkus "
"extension] provides metrics from automatic instrumentation implemented in "
"Quarkus and its extensions."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"There are multiple ways to output Micrometer metrics. Next there are some "
"examples:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Using the Micrometer Prometheus registry"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is the most common way to output metrics from Micrometer and the "
"default way in Quarkus. The Micrometer Prometheus registry will publish data "
"in the `/q/metrics` endpoint and a scraper inside the Grafana LGTM Dev "
"Service will grab it (*pull* data from the service)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Using the Micrometer OTLP registry"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The https://docs.quarkiverse.io/quarkus-micrometer-registry/dev/micrometer-"
"registry-otlp.html[Quarkiverse Micrometer OTLP registry] will output data "
"using the OpenTelemetry OTLP protocol to the Grafana LGTM Dev Service. This "
"will *push* data out of the service:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When using the https://micrometer.io/[Micrometer's] Quarkiverse OTLP "
"registry to push metrics to Grafana OTel LGTM, the `quarkus.micrometer."
"export.otlp.url` property is automatically set to OTel collector endpoint as "
"seen from the outside of the Docker container."
msgstr ""

msgid "OpenTelemetry"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"With OpenTelemetry, metrics, traces and logs can be created and sent to the "
"Grafana LGTM Dev Service."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default, the https://quarkus.io/guides/opentelemetry[OpenTelemetry "
"extension] will produce https://quarkus.io/guides/opentelemetry-"
"tracing[traces]. https://quarkus.io/guides/opentelemetry-metrics[Metrics] "
"and https://quarkus.io/guides/opentelemetry-logging[logs] must be enabled "
"separately."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `quarkus-opentelemetry` extension can be added to your build file like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `quarkus.otel.exporter.otlp.endpoint` property is automatically set to "
"the OTel collector endpoint as seen from the outside of the Docker container."
""
msgstr ""

msgid "The `quarkus.otel.exporter.otlp.protocol` is set to `http/protobuf`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Micrometer to OpenTelemetry bridge"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This extension provides Micrometer metrics and OpenTelemetry metrics, traces "
"and logs. All data is managed and sent out by the OpenTelemetry extension."
msgstr ""

msgid "**All signals are enabled by default.**"
msgstr ""

msgid "The extension can be added to your build file like this:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Grafana"
msgstr ""

msgid "Grafana UI access"
msgstr ""

msgid "Once you start your app in dev mode:"
msgstr ""

msgid "You will see a log entry like this:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Remember that Grafana is accessible in an ephemeral port, so you need to "
"check the logs to see which port is being used. In this example, the grafana "
"endpoint is `grafana.endpoint=http://localhost:42797`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Another option is to use the *Dev UI* (http://localhost:8080/q/dev-ui/"
"extensions) as the Grafana URL link will be available and if selected it "
"will open a new browser tab directly to the running Grafana instance:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Explore"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the explore section, you can query the data for all the data sources."
msgstr ""

msgid "To see traces, select the `tempo` data source and query for data:"
msgstr ""

msgid "For logs, select the `loki` data source and query for data:"
msgstr ""

msgid "The dashboards"
msgstr ""

msgid "The Dev Service includes a set of dashboards."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each dashboard is tuned for the specific application setup. The available "
"dashboards are:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Quarkus Micrometer OpenTelemetry*: to be used with the Micrometer and "
"OpenTelemetry extension."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Quarkus Micrometer OTLP registry*: to be used with the Micrometer OTLP "
"registry extension."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Quarkus Micrometer Prometheus registry*: to be used with the Micrometer "
"Prometheus registry extension."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Quarkus OpenTelemetry Logging*: to view logs coming from the OpenTelemetry "
"extension."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Some panels in the dashboards might take a few minutes to show accurate data "
"when their values are calculated over a sliding  time window."
msgstr ""

msgid "Custom dashboards"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Users can add their own Grafana dashboards with their own configuration.\n"
"Place a `grafana-dashboard-[your name].json` file into the `META-INF/"
"grafana` directory in your application, and LGTM Dev Services will pick it "
"up automatically, and include this dashboard configuration in the overall "
"Grafana dashboards YAML configuration."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For instance, the `/META-INF/grafana/grafana-dashboard-my-simple-prometheus-"
"dashboard.json` configuration file creates a dashboard named `My Simple "
"Prometheus Dashboard`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Additional configuration"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This extension will configure your `quarkus-opentelemetry` and `quarkus-"
"micrometer-registry-otlp` extensions to send data to the OTel Collector "
"bundled with the Grafana OTel LGTM image."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you don't want all the hassle with Dev Services (e.g. lookup and re-use "
"of existing running containers, etc) you can simply disable Dev Services and "
"enable just Dev Resource usage:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default Quarkus lets Testcontainers expose random ports for Grafana and "
"OTel, but you can force the usage of user-side fixed ports via setting the "
"ports yourself - but then be aware of port conflicts."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Internal container ports stay the same; e.g. 3000 for Grafana, 4317 for OTel "
"gRPC, 4318 for OTel HTTP, etc"
msgstr ""

msgid "Disabling observability Dev Services"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Observability Dev Services are enabled by default in dev mode. To disable "
"them entirely (both in dev and test mode), preventing the Grafana LGTM "
"container from starting automatically, set:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This also disables the Dev UI card, the shared container discovery, compose "
"support and the OpenTelemetry override properties."
msgstr ""

msgid "Enabling observability Dev Services in tests"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"By default, observability Dev Services do not start in test mode. To enable "
"the Grafana LGTM container during tests, set:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This property has no effect in dev mode, where Dev Services are controlled "
"solely by `quarkus.observability.enabled`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For the least 'auto-magical' usage in the tests, you can explicitly list "
"LGTM Dev Resource in the test as a `@QuarkusTestResource` resource:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Dev Resources mode"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"As an alternative to full Dev Services, you can enable a simplified Dev "
"Resources mode. In this mode, containers are started lazily at runtime via a "
"MicroProfile `ConfigSource` instead of during the build phase. This means "
"there is no shared container discovery, no compose support, no Dev UI card "
"and no OpenTelemetry override properties injected."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`quarkus.observability.enabled` must be set to `false` when using Dev "
"Resources mode. The two modes are mutually exclusive."
msgstr ""

